I actually think there is a strong analogy here, as long as we distinguish it from a mathematical identity. It fits several themes you’ve been exploring.

One way to think about it is this:

* Prime factorization gives the canonical decomposition of a number. There is exactly one.
* Partitions give the space of possible decompositions. There are usually many.

That distinction maps surprisingly well onto language.

Mathematics	Possible Pantheon analogue
Prime factorization	Canonical semantic primitives or invariants
Partition	A particular framing, explanation, narrative, or decomposition
Set of all partitions	The semantic possibility space for expressing an idea

For example, suppose the underlying invariant is “leadership.”

You can partition that concept into:

* courage + competence + trust
* vision + communication + execution
* responsibility + influence
* military command + organizational design + psychology

None of these is the unique decomposition. They are different useful partitions of the same semantic territory.

That resonates with several ideas you’ve developed:

* Semantic differentiation — intentionally splitting overloaded concepts.
* Representation independence — the invariant remains while the rendering changes.
* Indigo Hammer — split, compress, preserve interfaces, recombine.
* Semantic zoom — different partitions become useful at different resolutions.

So you might distinguish between:

* Primitive operators (your “primes”)
* Partition operators (ways of slicing the same semantic object)

Those are different jobs.

