Another interesting analogy

Natural language itself behaves much more like partitions than factorization.

A sentence isn’t usually generated from one unique decomposition.

Take:

“The door is open.”

Depending on context, you can partition its meaning into:

* physical state
* invitation
* security concern
* tactical opportunity
* metaphor

Same words.

Different semantic partitions.

Meaning depends on which partition of context the listener chooses.

